    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>In the architectural foundations of Western literary criticism, Aristotle&rsquo;s <i>Poetics</i> stands as a vigorous defense of dramatic art against the ascetic skepticism of his teacher Plato. In <i>The Republic</i>, Plato had famously banished dramatic poets from the ideal commonwealth, arguing that theatrical mimesis unwholesomely flatters and inflames the irrational, passionate dimensions of the human soul while corroding civic rationality. In sharp contrast, Aristotle rehabilitates tragedy as a vital cognitive and philosophical endeavor. For Aristotle, tragedy is not merely sensationalist entertainment, but the serious imitation (<i>mimesis</i>) of an elevated, complete action possessing magnitude. Through the structured elicitation of pity (<i>eleos</i>) and fear (<i>phobos</i>), tragic performance accomplishes the <i>catharsis</i> of these very emotions. Far from unhinging civic virtue, the aesthetic experience of tragedy serves a vital therapeutic and stabilizing function, restoring proper psychological equilibrium to the spectator.</p>\n<p>Crucial to Aristotle&rsquo;s dramaturgy is the specific moral configuration of the tragic protagonist. The ideal hero, he insists, must be neither an immaculate saint of unblemished virtue nor an unmitigated scoundrel of depraved malice. The downfall of a wholly blameless character would not arouse pity and fear, but merely outrage and moral revulsion; conversely, the ruin of an irredeemable villain satisfies a primitive craving for poetic justice without engaging our deeper human sympathies. The protagonist must therefore be an eminent, generally virtuous figure whose catastrophic collapse is precipitated by <i>hamartia</i>. While early modern commentators frequently mistranslated this term as a &ldquo;fatal character flaw&rdquo; or cardinal sin, classical philology demonstrates that <i>hamartia</i> originally denoted an &ldquo;erroneous judgment&rdquo; or missing the mark&mdash;an epistemological miscalculation executed under conditions of mortal finitude and imperfect foresight. The emotional momentum of the drama achieves maximum velocity when this misstep triggers <i>peripeteia</i> (an ironic, sudden reversal of fortune) culminating in <i>anagnorisis</i> (the profound cognitive transition from ignorance to harrowing awareness), famously dramatized in Oedipus&rsquo;s horrific recognition of his true parentage.</p>\n<p>The precise philosophical mechanism of <i>catharsis</i> has provoked centuries of intense scholarly debate. In the nineteenth century, classical scholar Jacob Bernays advanced an influential medical-homeopathic reading: tragedy acts as an emotional purgative, safely flushing accumulated, toxic passions from the spectator&rsquo;s psyche to yield tranquil relief. By the mid-twentieth century, however, scholars such as Gerald Else and Leon Golden contested this physiological model, advancing a cognitive interpretation. They argued that <i>catharsis</i> denotes not an evacuation of feelings, but the &ldquo;clarification&rdquo; of pity and fear. Through the structured symmetry of plot, raw personal anxieties are harmonized into an enlightened contemplation of universal human vulnerability and the inescapable limits of mortal self-determination. Philosopher Martha Nussbaum reinforces this view, proposing that tragedy educates our moral imagination by acknowledging that human flourishing is inherently fragile and perpetually vulnerable to uncontrolled circumstance.</p>\n<p>Despite Aristotle&rsquo;s enduring prestige, modern dramatists have sharply interrogated the political ideology latent within tragic closure. By framing immense suffering as the inexorable outcome of cosmic destiny or existential limits, does classical tragedy reconcile audiences to social injustices, fostering a passive fatalism? The twentieth-century Marxist playwright Bertolt Brecht vehemently repudiated Aristotelian catharsis, claiming that emotional purge dulls the spectator&rsquo;s critical faculties, transforming citizens into docile consumers who accept suffering as immutable nature. Brecht developed his &ldquo;epic theater&rdquo; precisely to disrupt cathartic empathy through alienation effects, compelling spectators to analyze structural socioeconomic causes rather than weeping over cosmic fate. Nevertheless, Aristotle&rsquo;s core formulation remains compelling: by engaging the catastrophic missteps of flawed heroes, tragedy compels us to confront our shared precariousness.</p>",
