    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;33)</div>\n<p>Emerging in the late eighteenth century alongside the architectural and literary experiments of Horace Walpole, Ann Radcliffe, Matthew Lewis, and Mary Shelley, the Gothic sensibility developed as the dark shadow of the European Enlightenment. While Enlightenment philosophy proclaimed the inevitable triumph of secular rationalism, empirical science, and intellectual transparency, the Gothic probed the subterranean realms of irrational dread, ancestral transgression, somatic decay, and taboo desire. The crumbling medieval castles, claustrophobic subterranean crypts, and labyrinthine corridors that populated Gothic fiction were never mere sensationalist stage props. Rather, they functioned as profound spatial allegories for psychological disturbance and historical trauma. By staging the violent return of forgotten crimes, tyrannical patriarchs, and buried bloodlines, Gothic narratives suggested that the rational modern subject remained hopelessly haunted by feudal barbarism and unresolved psychic guilt.</p>\n<p>The psychological underpinnings of this dark aesthetic received their definitive theoretical formulation in Sigmund Freud&rsquo;s 1919 essay, <i>Das Unheimliche</i> (The Uncanny). Freud initiated his inquiry through a meticulous linguistic excavation of the German adjective <i>heimlich</i>. In its primary usage, <i>heimlich</i> denotes that which is homely, familiar, intimate, and comfortably domestic; however, across historical usage, its meaning inverted to signify that which is concealed, secret, and kept out of sight. Its apparent antithesis, <i>unheimlich</i> (unhomely or uncanny), thus shares an intimate etymological convergence with its opposite. Drawing upon the philosopher Friedrich Schelling&rsquo;s dictum that &ldquo;uncanny is what one calls everything that was meant to remain secret and hidden and has come into the open,&rdquo; Freud posited that uncanny terror is not aroused by something utterly novel, alien, or incomprehensible. Rather, the uncanny is an experience in which something once intimately familiar and anciently ingrained in the psyche, having undergone severe psychological repression, resurfaces in an estranged and terrifying guise&mdash;a phenomenon Freud termed &ldquo;the return of the repressed.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Freud illustrated the uncanny through classic Gothic motifs, most notably the Doppelg&auml;nger (the double), lifelike automata, and haunted domestic spaces. In analyzing E.T.A. Hoffmann&rsquo;s dark Romantic tale <i>The Sandman</i>, Freud observed that dread emerges when the boundary between animate life and inanimate machinery collapses, reawakening archaic infantile terrors regarding bodily integrity and castration. Similarly, the figure of the double&mdash;extensively studied by psychoanalyst Otto Rank&mdash;undergoes a tragic psychological reversal across human development. In early childhood and primitive culture, the double originated as an energetic narcissistic defense against mortal annihilation: an immortal second self or protective guardian spirit. Yet under the regime of the adult conscience and ego-ideal, this preservation mechanism mutates into its horrifying opposite, transforming into an ominous harbinger of mortality, internal fragmentation, and moral self-recrimination. The haunted castle functions as the architectural externalization of this mental state: a polite, modern surface concealing dark, repressed instinctual forces buried beneath the floorboards.</p>\n<p>While Freud located the uncanny primarily within individual infantile complexes, contemporary literary and cultural theorists have expanded the concept into the collective sociopolitical sphere. Feminist scholars, such as Sandra Gilbert and Susan Gubar, argue that the Gothic uncanny reflects the institutional repression of female autonomy in patriarchal society, epitomized by the trope of the &ldquo;madwoman in the attic.&rdquo; Concurrently, postcolonial theorists contend that the haunting of domestic European manors reflects the repressed colonial guilt of empire, wherein the exploited colonized subject returns to disrupt imperial peace. Through these interventions, the Gothic uncanny reveals itself not merely as a clinical symptom of individual neurosis, but as a crucial cultural index registering that which Enlightenment society attempted to bury, but could never permanently erase.</p>",
